  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Alaska has $3.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 15.0% year over year. Award activity reached 8,944 contracts with an average award size of $366K. Fiscal totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress, and those totals reflect obligations recorded so far. Construction, health benefits administration, and facilities support are the largest industry categories by dollar value in Alaska. The Department of Defense is the largest buying agency with $1.3B in obligations in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $525M and the General Services Administration with $495M. Top vendors include DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. with $470M and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP with $288M. Historical context shows fiscal obligations varying from $2.3B in FY21 to a peak of $3.9B in FY25, then returning to $3.3B in FY26 to date. These figures describe current federal contract flows in Alaska and highlight where procurement dollars concentrate across agencies, vendors, and industries.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Alaska changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 15.0% year over year. AwardCount reached 8,944 and averageAwardSize was $366K in FY2026 to date. Fiscal history shows obligations moved from $2.3B in FY21 up to $3.9B in FY25, then back to $3.3B in FY26 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies, industries, and vendors dominate federal spending in Alaska?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $1.3B in FY2026 to date, followed by the Department of Homeland Security with $525M and the General Services Administration with $495M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry with $969M, followed by Pharmacy Benefit Management and Other Third Party Administration of Insurance and Pension Funds with $288M and Facilities Support Services with $220M. DAVIS CONSTRUCTORS & ENGINEERS, INC. leads vendors with $470M, followed by TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP with $288M and TUTOR PERINI CORPORATION with $144M."
      }
    ]
  },
